(3t-6{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ (2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})t+(-4{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot column is the $1$st one. Hence, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the $1$st equation, we get\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, for any ${x}_{2}\\ne 0,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{1}}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2})}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535121 true] [17592186332186 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332187 13194139535121 true] [17592186332186 1089 "q-107018" 13194139535121 true] [17592186332186 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535121 true] [17592186332186 1100 "Consider the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ of polynomials from $R[t],$ where\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=-1+t-2{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=-2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=t-2{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}$ from a non-zero solution of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}\\cdot {p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}\\cdot {p}_{3}(t)=0.$" 13194139535121 true] [17592186332186 1113 #uuid "392cc0b6-5e02-5f4f-a072-02ad7a035801" 13194139535121 true] [17592186332186 1145 "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t)=0.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for ${p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),$ and ${p}_{3}(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-1+t-2{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(-2)+{x}_{3}(t-2{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ (-{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})+({x}_{1}+{x}_{3})t+(-2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}){t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd. Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substitute it into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}-2(\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3})=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, for any ${x}_{3}\\ne 0,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{-{x}_{3}}{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3})}=-2.\n$$" 13194139535121 true] [17592186332187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332188 13194139535121 true] [17592186332187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332189 13194139535121 